<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="diterpene phytoalexins precursors biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-2981" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Phytoalexins are low-molecular weight compounds that are synthesized and accumulated in plants after exposure to microorganisms. They have been suggested to serve as plant antibiotics in the defense systems of plants |CITS: [11149940]|. Biosynthesis of phytoalexins is induced in infected leaves of rice plants. Four structurally distinct types of polycyclic diterpenes, oryzalexins A-F, (-)-phytocassanes A-E, momilactones A and B, and oryzalexin S, have been identified as phytoalexins in the infected rice leaves. The diterpenes &lt;i&gt;ent&lt;/i&gt;-cassa-12,15-diene, &lt;i&gt;ent&lt;/i&gt;-sandaracopimara-8(14),15-diene, 9&amp;beta;-pimara-7,15-diene and stemar-13-ene are thought to be the precursors to phytocassanes A-E, oryzalexins A-F, momilactones A and B and oryzalexin S, respectively |CITS: [14675427]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; This branched pathways starts with the cyclization of geranylgeranyl diphosphate into &lt;i&gt;ent&lt;/i&gt;-copalyl diphosphate and &lt;i&gt;syn&lt;/i&gt;-copalyl diphosphate. The catalytic conversion of these two compounds into the four diterpene hydrocarbons is achieved by diterpene cyclases thought to play key roles in the biosynthesis of rice diterpenoid phytoalexins. The metabolic fate of the ambiguous |FRAME:CPD-9907| remains to be elucidated. However, it has been demonstrated that the cytochrome P450 dependent monooxygenase (CYP99A3) (see |FRAME:PWY-7477|) was able to catalyze the successive oxidation of |FRAME:CPD-9907| yielding |FRAME:CPD-16681|. This oxidation cascade comprised the conversion of the C19 methyl group (|FRAME:CPD-9907|) into a carboxylic acid group (|FRAME:CPD-16681|) via the intermediate alcohol (|FRAME:CPD-16679|) and aldehyde (|FRAME:CPD-16680|). Although those compounds have not yet been identified in &lt;i&gt;Oryza&lt;/i&gt; the metabolic activity of CYP99A3 indicates the direction of possible biosynthetic pathways |CITS:[21175892]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="nilo-poyanco" />
  </Authors>
